<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bathsheba</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bathsheba was the wife of Uriah the Hittite who, according to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a>, became the wife of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">King David</a> after he committed adultery with her and arranged Uriah's death in battle. She was the mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a> and, with the prophet Nathan, secured his succession to the throne against his elder half-brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/adonijah">Adonijah</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bathsheba.jpg" alt="Bathsheba" width="320" height="381" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Willem Drost · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Willem_Drost_-_Batsheba_met_de_brief_van_koning_David.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Bathsheba is called the daughter of Eliam in 2 Samuel 11:3 and the daughter of Ammiel in 1 Chronicles 3:5, where she is named Bathshua. Because a man named Eliam is listed in 2 Samuel 23:34 as a son of Ahithophel, a counsellor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>, Jewish tradition and several scholars take her to be Ahithophel's granddaughter, though some doubt that Ahithophel was old enough. According to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> she had five children with David, among them Shimea, Shobab, Nathan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>.</p><p>The account in 2 Samuel 11 says <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> saw her bathing from the roof of his palace, learned that she was Uriah's wife, and slept with her. When Uriah, away on campaign, would not go home to his wife, David ordered his general <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joab">Joab</a> to place him on the front lines, and Uriah was killed during the siege of Rabbah. Bathsheba mourned him, and David then took her into his house.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a>'s act displeased God, who sent the prophet Nathan to reprove him with a parable of a rich man who took a poor man's lamb. David confessed, but the first child born to Bathsheba fell ill and died after seven days. She later bore <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/solomon">Solomon</a>. In David's old age she secured Solomon's succession to the throne in place of David's older surviving sons, on the strength of promises David had made to her. Nathan's prophecy was held to be fulfilled when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/absalom">Absalom</a> rebelled and took ten of his father's concubines in public.</p><p>Scholars disagree on how to read her part. Some, among them David J. Zucker, see her as a victim of what he calls power rape, and Michael D. Coogan notes that the text faults David from its first verse; Kenneth E. Bailey argues instead that she displayed herself deliberately. The story affected how David's later decline was understood. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> she appears in the genealogy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jesus">Jesus</a> in Matthew 1:6 and in medieval typology is linked with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mary-mother-of-jesus">Virgin Mary</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-orthodox-church">Ethiopian Orthodox Church</a> venerates her as a saint. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a> does not mention her in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quran">Quran</a>, and some tradition rejects the story as incompatible with the infallibility of prophets. Artists often showed her at her bath, as in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rembrandt">Rembrandt</a>'s painting in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louvre">Louvre</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bathsheba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Kingship was dynastic but contested: the succession to David was fought out among his sons, and Solomon secured the throne around 970 BCE through the support of the prophet Nathan, the priest Zadok and his mother <b>Bathsheba</b> against his older brother Adonijah.</blockquote><a href="/empire/united-kingdom-of-israel">United Kingdom of Israel · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
